{"step_index":115,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-30T05:54:13Z","thinking":"Initial state management requires adding new properties. These properties will track notification-related settings like the reminder interval, visibility of the notification bar, and whether desktop notifications are enabled, alongside cycle and alert management. Implement the logic to correctly load and save these new state variables during application initialization and shutdown respectively.\n\n\nLoading notification bar visibility and desktop notification enablement from parsed data is confirmed. The next step involves examining the computation of active alerts, notification bar rendering, and the periodic reminder execution flow. Designing the `computePendingAlerts()` function now includes analyzing pending assignments, sorting by due date, and generating alerts based on time remaining relative to the deadline.\n\n\nNow focusing on implementing the assignment and attendance alert logic. Assignments are being processed to determine urgency based on time remaining until the deadline, generating alerts with varying urgency levels. Attendance data is analyzed to identify potential shortages, producing appropriate alerts when attendance falls below a specified threshold.\n\n\nNow, the code examines attendance records, generating critical alerts when attendance falls below a dangerous threshold. A separate warning alert is issued if attendance is within a warning range, indicating the need for immediate improvement. Next, upcoming exams within a specific timeframe are processed, sorting exams by date to identify the closest upcoming exams.\n\n\nExam alerts are being generated based on proximity to the exam date, with distinct messages and urgency levels assigned depending on the days remaining. The logic now handles upcoming exams, creating alerts that direct users to either review the syllabus or prepare for the exam depending on the time left.  Now inspecting the function responsible for rendering the notification bar with its timers and content display.\n\n\nThe notification bar's visibility is controlled by a state variable; when the state indicates invisibility, the bar is hidden and vice-versa.  The number of unread alerts is updated in the header. If no alerts are present, the bar displays a celebratory message and visual theme; otherwise, it displays an action button.\n\nThe current alert index within the available alerts array is validated to ensure it remains within bounds, resetting to 0 if out of range and handling negative indexes by wrapping around. The current alert is then retrieved from the available list of alerts by using the validated index, which is controlled by another state variable.\n\n\nThe rendering logic sets notification bar styles and content based on alert urgency. Critical alerts trigger an urgent-themed bar and badge, warnings/urgent alerts use a warning theme, and all others use a default style. The notification bar is populated with alert details including the icon, badge, message, time remaining, and action button.\n\nThe next and previous functions cycle through available alerts. The current alert index is incremented or decremented, ensuring it stays within array bounds using the modulo operator for looping. Then, the timer is reset and the notification bar re-rendered with the new alert content.\n\n\nThe \"Notif\" function decrements the current alert index, looping back to the end if necessary, then resets the cycle timer and re-renders the bar. \"handleNotifBarAction\" navigates the user to an action tab when an alert with a specified tab is clicked. \"dismissNotifBar\" hides the bar, updates state, and displays a confirmation message. \"showNotifBarAgain\" restores the bar's visibility, updates the state, and displays a success message. \"toggleNotifCycle\" toggles auto-cycle state and updates the button text and displays an info message.\nThe auto-cycle function, if active, clears any existing intervals. It then starts a new interval to update a progress bar based on cycle duration. The interval updates progress and the progress bar width accordingly, but only when the cycle is active.\n\n\nThe auto-cycle function's internal timer increments a progress variable and updates a visual progress bar. This occurs repeatedly as long as the auto-cycle is active and stops otherwise. The timer resets the progress bar to 0 at the end of each cycle and restarts.\n\n\nDesktop notifications are generated only if the browser supports them and the user has granted permission.  If enabled, a new notification is created with the alert's message, an icon, and a badge.  If the user denies permission, a message is displayed indicating the lack of support, but in-app reminders remain active. When the notification bar is displayed, the logic now increments the alert index, ensuring the application cycles through all alerts. Upon permission, the system saves the enabled state and updates UI elements.\n\nDesktop notification permission request is initiated if the browser supports it. Upon granting permission, a desktop notification is created using the current alert's data; otherwise, an in-app message provides feedback.\n\nWhen the notification center is toggled, the reminder interval select value is populated. If desktop notifications are supported and permitted, the button indicating this becomes active.\n\nThe notification center list is rendered by clearing the existing content. If no alerts exist, a celebratory message is displayed; otherwise, each pending alert is rendered as a list item.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"C:\\Users\\DELL\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\student-survival-app\\app.js","EndLine":2356,"StartLine":2280,"toolAction":"Checking app.js end","toolSummary":"View end of app.js"}}]}
